draft_notes.explanation:
Original mechanism: each grey band is a stack of t×t squares. Square i is placed one step further along the original downward direction and one step left or right. Later squares overwrite earlier ones. Square i is coloured legend[i mod n], where the legend is read in the original left-to-right direction. A cell therefore takes the colour of the largest square index covering it, d = min(p, q, cap):
- p is the offset from the band's starting edge along the stacking axis;
- q is the offset from the side where the first slice touches the bbox;
- cap = extent along the stacking axis minus w, where w is the first slice's length.

The factory maps the original 'down' and 'right' unit vectors through the D4 transform: flip the column sign for indices >= 4, then apply (dr, dc) -> (dc, -dr) once per clockwise turn. The image of 'down' gives the visible stacking axis and sign. This tells the program which bbox edge holds the starting slice. The image of 'right' gives the visible direction in which to read the legend; legend cells are sorted by their projection onto that vector.

Colour roles are fixed constants: background = palette[0] and band colour = palette[5]. Every other non-background cell is a legend cell. The sideways direction of each band is not fixed by D4. It is inferred per object, from which bbox side the first slice touches along the cross axis. Each band is a 4-connected component of the band colour and is recoloured in place. Everything else is copied unchanged.
